    "created_at": "2024-07-04T07:17:26Z",
    "updated_at": "2024-07-04T09:42:12Z",
    "body": "The `<dialog>` element currently has a `close` [event](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/HTMLDialogElement/close_event) that authors can use to know that a dialog has closed (and run some code like removing event listeners).\r\nA corresponding `open` event does not yet exist (although I hoped it would be added to the spec eventually).\r\nHow are the new `toggle` events related to the (already existing) `close` event and the (hypothetical) `open` event?\r\n\r\nAm I correct to assume that a `ToggleEvent: newState=closed` would be equivalent to the `close` event for all intents and purposes and therefore it wouldn't really matter which one I would use? (edit: it seems there might be a slight difference in how they are [raised by the platform](https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/9046) (synchronous or via microtask); but this should not make a practical difference to consumers of these events)\r\nAnd that a `ToggleEvent: newState=open` would be equivalent to the hypothetical `open` event?\r\nAnd finally that the existence of a `ToggleEvent` would crush the last remaining hope that we will ever get a symmetrical `open` event corresponding to the `close` event?",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
    "reactions": {
      "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/whatwg/html/issues/comments/2208281525/reactions",
